\section{Summary}\label{sec:compiler-db-summary}

Viewing the compiler as a database---with the IR as a collection of relations, analyses as queries, and transformations as query-driven rewrites---offers a fundamentally different approach to compiler architecture. It enables natural incrementality, persistence, and parallelism, and provides a declarative interface for expressing analyses and optimizations. While the approach is still largely research, it points toward a future where compilers are more modular, more maintainable, and more responsive to changes.

\begin{historical}
The idea of representing programs as databases has appeared in various forms over the decades. The Program Database (PDB) at Microsoft, used by Visual Studio's C++ compiler, stores program information in a persistent database for incremental compilation and debugging. The Datalog-based approach to program analysis (Whaley et al., 2004) demonstrated the scalability of declarative analysis. The relational view of compilation has been explored in research systems like Optix and in the emerging field of ``compiler as a service.'' The convergence of database technology (with its mature query optimization, indexing, and transaction support) and compiler technology is a promising direction for future compiler architectures.
\end{historical}
